Let  

- \(v\) = Grandma’s speed on the beach (miles per minute)  
- \(2v\) = Grandma’s speed on the sidewalk (twice the beach speed)

**1. Find the beach speed \(v\).**  
Grandma spends 40 minutes on the beach and walks 2 miles there.  
\[
v=\frac{\text{distance on beach}}{\text{time on beach}}
   =\frac{2\text{ miles}}{40\text{ min}}
   =\frac{2}{40}\text{ miles per minute}
   =\frac{1}{20}\text{ miles per minute}
\]

**2. Find the sidewalk speed.**  
\[
\text{Sidewalk speed}=2v=2\left(\frac{1}{20}\right)=\frac{1}{10}\text{ miles per minute}
\]

**3. Compute the time on the sidewalk.**  
The sidewalk segment is 1 mile long.  
\[
\text{Time on sidewalk}=\frac{\text{distance}}{\text{speed}}
   =\frac{1\text{ mile}}{1/10\text{ miles per minute}}
   =10\text{ minutes}
\]

**4. Total time for the 3‑mile walk.**  
\[
\text{Total time}= \text{time on beach} + \text{time on sidewalk}
   = 40\text{ min} + 10\text{ min}
   = 50\text{ minutes}
\]

Answer: 50

